==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|schaply}}, {{m|enm|schappely|t=well-shaped, beautiful, attractive}}, perhaps from {{inh|en|ang|*ġesċeaplīċ|t=according to design, proper, fit}} (suggested by {{cog|ang|ġesċeaplīċe|t=properly, fitting, well}}), equivalent to {{suf|en|shape|ly|id2=adjectival}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-shapely.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er|more}}

# Having a [[pleasing]] [[shape]], [[pleasant]] to [[look]] at.
#* {{RQ:Heller Catch-22|The Soldier in White|171|The more solicitous of the two was Nurse Cramer, a '''shapely''', pretty, sexless girl with a wholesome unattractive face.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=1 September 2020|work=The Guardian|author=Tom Lamont|url=https://www.theguardian.com/news/2020/sep/01/frank-fisher-butchers-shop-closure-death-of-high-street|title=The butcher's shop that lasted 300 years (give or take)
|passage=It used to be, whenever Frank passed a rival butcher’s window (and later the curved glass of a supermarket meat counter), he would peer in unjealously, looking for the subtleties that spoke of talent with a knife. Were their fillets '''shapely'''? }}

